Section 11
License Issuance Authority Implementation Contract
========================================================

11.1 Purpose

This section defines the first implementation contract for the USOP License
Issuance Authority.

The License Issuance Authority is vendor/operator-side commercial tooling.

It is not customer runtime functionality.

The License Issuance Authority must never be exposed as a customer-accessible
license issuance API.

The USOP customer runtime verifies licenses.

The USOP License Authority issues licenses.

These responsibilities remain separate.


11.2 License Artifact Contract

The canonical USOP License artifact is a UTF-8 JSON document whose top-level
object conforms exactly to the LicenseInstallRequest contract accepted by the
USOP runtime.

The artifact must contain the complete signed License envelope required for
local validation.

The initial implementation must not introduce an additional proprietary binary
wrapper, JWT representation, encrypted container, or second canonical License
format.

Distribution filenames are operational metadata and are not cryptographic
truth.

A recommended human-readable filename convention is:

<license-identifier>.license.json


11.3 License Format Version

The initial canonical License artifact format version is:

1.0

License format version describes the License artifact contract.

It is independent of:

- USOP product version;
- this specification's document version;
- commercial edition;
- commercial purpose;
- signing-key version.


11.4 Cryptographic Signing Contract

The License Authority must use the same canonicalization contract enforced by
the USOP runtime.

The authoritative signing input is the deterministic UTF-8 JSON representation
of canonical_payload produced by the canonical License payload serializer.

The License Authority must:

1. canonicalize canonical_payload;
2. calculate the lowercase SHA-256 digest of those canonical bytes;
3. place that digest in canonical_payload_hash;
4. sign the exact canonical bytes using ECDSA P-256 with SHA-256;
5. encode the ASN.1 DER ECDSA signature using Base64;
6. identify the signing key using signing_key_identifier.

The License Authority must reuse the canonical USOP License canonicalization
implementation rather than creating an independent serialization algorithm.

The corresponding customer runtime must validate the artifact using trusted
vendor-controlled public verification material.


11.5 License Identifier

License identifiers are assigned exclusively by the License Authority.

The initial identifier contract is an opaque vendor-generated identifier with
the form:

USOP-LIC-<UUID>

The UUID establishes uniqueness.

Commercial meaning must not be encoded into the License identifier.

The identifier must not encode:

- customer name;
- organization name;
- commercial edition;
- commercial purpose;
- seat limit;
- expiration;
- deployment;
- commercial modules;
- feature entitlements.

Commercial changes result in a new immutable License rather than reinterpretation
of an existing License identifier.


11.6 Organization Binding

Every issued License must bind to exactly one canonical USOP Organization.

organization_id is therefore mandatory commercial identity within the signed
License contract.

Organization binding is independent of external provider tenancy such as
Microsoft Entra tenants, AWS accounts, GitHub organizations, or other provider
environments.


11.7 Deployment Binding

Deployment binding remains optional.

A License may contain:

deployment_identifier = null

when commercial entitlement is intentionally Organization-bound rather than
Deployment-bound.

The first Design Partner License may use Organization-only binding.

The License Authority must not invent, derive, or synthesize a Deployment
Identifier from:

- hostname;
- container identifier;
- MAC address;
- CPU identifier;
- virtual machine identifier;
- Kubernetes pod identifier;
- other transient infrastructure identity.

Architecture reconciliation note:

Section 8.2 defines Deployment Identifiers as stable identifiers generated and
preserved by USOP.

The current Organization creation implementation permits a deployment_identifier
to be supplied by the Organization creation caller and does not yet demonstrate
the canonical USOP-generated Deployment Identifier lifecycle described by
Section 8.2.

This discrepancy must be reconciled in a separate focused increment.

It must not be silently resolved inside License issuance logic.

Until that lifecycle is explicitly implemented and validated, Organization-only
License binding is the preferred first Design Partner contract.


11.8 Commercial Edition and Purpose

Commercial Edition and Commercial Purpose remain independent dimensions.

Edition describes commercial packaging.

Purpose describes why the License was issued.

The License Authority must not infer runtime capability solely from Commercial
Edition.

Commercial modules and feature entitlements remain explicit signed fields.

A Design Partner may therefore receive, for example:

commercial_edition = Enterprise
commercial_purpose = Beta

without creating special-case Beta authorization logic.


11.9 Effective and Expiration Dates

License effective and expiration dates are issuance inputs.

The core License issuance service must not hard-code a permanent Beta,
Evaluation, Partner, or Production duration.

Operator tooling may provide convenience defaults.

The initial Design Partner operational process may use a 90-day Beta License,
but 90 days is not an immutable architectural property of Beta licensing.


11.10 Seat Limit

Seat limit is explicit signed commercial data.

The License Authority must not infer seat count from Commercial Edition or
Commercial Purpose.

A finite seat limit may be used for the first Design Partner License.

The initial operational recommendation is 10 seats unless the Design Partner
requires a different explicitly approved value.


11.11 Commercial Modules and Feature Entitlements

Commercial modules and feature entitlements must represent concrete licensed
capabilities.

The License Authority must not populate future or unimplemented commercial
modules merely because they appear in product strategy or architecture
documentation.

USOP Core remains the required platform foundation.

Optional paid extensions remain explicitly licensed.

Null and empty collection representations must not be assigned inconsistent
commercial meaning.

The canonical representation used by the License Authority must be established
and regression-tested before the first production License artifact is issued.


11.12 Private Signing-Key Boundary

Private License signing material belongs exclusively to the vendor/operator
License Authority.

Private signing material must never:

- be committed to source control;
- be included in a customer Docker image;
- be included in a Design Partner distribution package;
- be accepted from a customer License installation request;
- be stored in the customer runtime database;
- be written to application logs;
- be exposed through a customer-accessible API;
- be represented in the runtime trusted public-key registry.

Issuer development and regression testing must use ephemeral test signing keys
until the real vendor signing authority is intentionally provisioned.


11.13 Runtime Trust Boundary

Customer runtime trust must remain vendor-controlled.

License installation must not allow the License artifact, browser, API caller,
customer configuration request, or other untrusted installation input to define
its own trust root.

The runtime trusted signing-key registry contains public verification material
only.

Unknown signing-key identifiers fail closed.

The real vendor public verification key must be provisioned through an explicit
release-controlled trust mechanism before a real Design Partner License can be
installed.


11.14 Initial Issuance Development Sequence

The initial License Authority should be developed incrementally:

1. private signing-key loading and validation contract;
2. License issuance service;
3. operator issuance CLI/tooling;
4. issuer/runtime interoperability and adversarial validation;
5. vendor public verification-key provisioning;
6. real vendor signing-authority bootstrap;
7. first Design Partner License issuance.

Real vendor private signing material should not be generated merely to support
development tests.

Ephemeral cryptographic keys should be used until the complete issuance and
verification path has passed regression and adversarial validation.


11.15 First Design Partner Operational Profile

The recommended initial Design Partner License profile is:

- License Format Version: 1.0
- Commercial Edition: Enterprise
- Commercial Purpose: Beta
- Organization Binding: required
- Deployment Binding: none initially
- Duration: approximately 90 days
- Seat Limit: 10 unless explicitly adjusted
- Commercial Modules: implemented licensed modules only
- Feature Entitlements: implemented licensed entitlements only

These values describe the initial Design Partner operating decision.

Except for License Format Version 1.0 and the architectural trust boundaries
defined above, they are not permanent commercial rules.

Future License issuance must remain capable of supporting Evaluation,
Production, Partner, renewal, replacement, additional commercial modules,
additional entitlements, and future provider/MSP commercial models without
redesigning the License trust chain.

